<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kahuripan</h1><p class="st-meta">1019 CE – 1045 CE · capital Kahuripan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The East Javanese kingdom of Airlangga, who rebuilt Javanese royal power after the fall of Medang in 1016.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When Haji Wurawari destroyed the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa Teguh</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom</a> in 1016, his young son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>, a prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, fled to the forest with a few followers. In 1019 priests consecrated him king, and over the next two decades he reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> from rival lords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pucangan-inscription">Pucangan inscription</a> of 1041 tells his story in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a>. He built dams on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas River</a>, encouraged trade through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hujung-galuh">Hujung Galuh</a>, and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist clergy. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjunawiwaha">Arjunawiwaha</a>, seen as an allegory of his reign.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Before retiring as an ascetic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> had his kingdom divided around 1045, tradition says by the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-bharada">Mpu Bharada</a>, between two sons: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/janggala">Janggala</a> in the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kediri-kingdom">Panjalu</a>, in the west. He died around 1049, and is believed to be buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candi-belahan">Belahan</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-penanggungan">Mount Penanggungan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kahuripan.jpg" alt="Kahuripan" width="320" height="143"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kediri_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1019 CE – 1045 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kahuripan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Airlangga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Kahuripan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1032&amp;focus=kahuripan" aria-label="Kahuripan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7674 2452 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kahuripan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7994 2708L8208 2754M7994 2708L7994 2708M7994 2708L8097 2725"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8208 2754h0M7994 2708h0M8097 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7994 2708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7994 2708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kahuripan. <a href="/atlas?year=1032&amp;focus=kahuripan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
